<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<Version>5.2.7-local.12</Version>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>v5.2.6 - FIX: the remaining per-call device allocations that made CUDA GRAPH CAPTURE IMPOSSIBLE. 5.2.5 fixed Conv1DKernel and listed six further sites carrying the same pattern; all are converted now - EIGHT sites across SIX kernels: ColorConversionKernel (2, and it sits in the DEPTH PIPELINE, which uses capture), TurboQuantKernels (2, fused + flash attention), ShortConvKernel (2, LFM2 decode), GatedDeltaNetKernel (1, qwen3.5 GatedDeltaNet), MissingElementWiseKernels (1, DepthToSpace and siblings), Conv1DKernel (1, fixed in 5.2.5). Each allocated a device params buffer on a path that runs per call. Allocate1D is a cuMemAlloc, which is ILLEGAL inside a CUDA graph-capture window and faults with an ACCESS VIOLATION that no try/catch can observe - the process simply dies - so capture was impossible for any graph containing one of these kernels. It also cost an allocation plus a host-to-device copy per call on EVERY backend, for values that are a pure function of the shapes. New Kernels/ParamBufferCache holds one device buffer per DISTINCT param set, reused for the kernel's life. TWO of these were worse than a capture blocker. MissingElementWiseKernels allocated AND THEN REWROTE the buffer - exactly the corruption its own comment warned about ("the command-encoder may still reference the prior _paramsBuf at next sync"); a cache hit writes nothing, so the hazard is removed rather than managed, and the exact-size SubView dance that existed to make the rewrite safe is gone with it. ShortConvKernel used a single "last values" slot, which is correct only while every node using the kernel shares one param set - an assumption about LFM2's layer uniformity that per-set caching does not need. That is the same trap that made the first attempt at the Conv1DKernel fix fail: with eighteen Conv nodes a warm pass ends with the LAST node's params resident and the next pass misses on its FIRST, which under capture is the fatal allocation. TurboQuantKernels had no Dispose at all, so its retired-buffer list was never freed for the life of the session; the cache is bounded by construction and disposed with the kernel. Reuse is safe where fresh-per-call was defensive: rewriting a params buffer that a PENDING dispatch still reads would corrupt it, but a cache HIT performs no write - the values are already the ones resident. The five allocations left in these files are capacity-guarded (FWHTKernel ping/pad buffers, MissingElementWiseKernels' top-k index buffer) and allocate only when a larger buffer is needed, which is not a per-call cost. Full six-backend sweep green. See Changelog.</PackageReleaseNotes>
		<GeneratePackageOnBuild>True</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<EmbedAllSources>true</EmbedAllSources>
		<DebugType>Embedded</DebugType>
		<Title>SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML</Title>
		<Authors>LostBeard</Authors>
		<Description>Hardware-agnostic machine learning infrastructure for .NET. Implements high-performance neural network layers in C# that transpile to WebGPU, CUDA, OpenCL, and WebGL via SpawnDev.ILGPU. Optimized for Blazor WebAssembly and native GPU execution.</Description>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML</PackageProjectUrl>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE.txt</PackageLicenseFile>
		<PackageIcon>icon-128.png</PackageIcon>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML.git</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<PackageTags>Blazor;BlazorWebAssembly;WebGPU;WebGL;WebAssembly;WebWorkers;GPGPU;ILGPU;ParallelProcessing;Cuda;OpenCL;ComputeShader;CrossPlatform;MachineLearning</PackageTags>
		<NoPackageAnalysis>true</NoPackageAnalysis>
		<EnableDefaultCompileItems>true</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
		<IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
		<LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
		<AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="Assets\icon-128.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\LICENSE.txt" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.SpawnJS" Version="2.1.9" />
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.ILGPU" Version="5.2.5-local.1" />
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.WebTorrent" Version="4.2.2" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<SupportedPlatform Include="browser" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="windows" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="linux" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="macos" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<EmbeddedResource Include="Data\imagenet_classes.txt" />
	</ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The MIT English frontend. ZipVoice cannot speak English without a phonemizer - its shipped lexicon
      is Chinese-only - and the reference implementation reaches for espeak-ng (GPL-3) at that point.
      This is dependency-free and browser-capable, so it costs nothing to carry.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SpawnDev.Phonemizer\SpawnDev.Phonemizer.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
